<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Sa'id Mirza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1424–1469</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Sa'id Mirza (1424–1469) was a Timurid ruler, a great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>, who seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> in 1451 with the help of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> khan Abu'l-Khayr and later took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, reuniting much of the Timurid realm. In 1469 he invaded western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> but was defeated and captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> and executed. He was the grandfather of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-said-mirza.jpg" alt="Abu Sa'id Mirza" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1459-60 artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jam%E2%80%99i_Jam_(The_Cup_of_Jamshid)_22.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
